 palps<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) B.J. Richardson (CSIRO)","path":"Images/features/Female%20palp/Tarsus%20shape/Female_palp_paddle.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Female%20palp/Tarsus%20shape/Female_palp_paddle.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":191,"uid":"07afdc28-a6e0-4891-943d-8a6aa4327514","name":"straight","type":0,"feature":80,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of straight emboli<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_%20form_%20straight.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_%20form_%20straight.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":192,"uid":"4d9983eb-8c62-48a2-820a-ed50065aef13","name":"curved","type":0,"feature":80,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of curved emboli<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Curve less than 90 degrees.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_curved.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_curved.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":193,"uid":"c216ec73-4bac-4db5-ba7a-0e7c6cd3b7c5","name":"coiled - less than one coil","type":0,"feature":80,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of a coiled embolus<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) B.J. Richardson (CSIRO, MP)","comments":"Coiled more than 90 degrees but less than 360 degrees.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_less_than_one_coil.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_less_than_one_coil.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":194,"uid":"c2fbd05c-c422-4f90-acb9-d888b92b5755","name":"coiled - distal or lateral to tegulum","type":0,"feature":80,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of single full-coil emboli distal or lateral to the tegulum<br/>Illustrators (and &copy;) ; M. Zabka (L) (QMB), B.J. Richardson (C, R) (CSIRO, MP)","comments":"Most genera follow the lower left diagram, however, occasionally, the embolus may lie over or beside the tegulum.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_%20distal.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_%20distal.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":195,"uid":"e0e4ef94-d01e-4736-a675-15d743da46c3","name":"coiled - long  looping coils around/over  tegulum","type":0,"feature":80,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of embolus following long loose coils around and over the tegulum<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB) (CSIRO)","comments":"The embolus is very long and follows a variable and convoluted route around and across the ventral surface of the tegulum.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_loosely_coiled.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_loosely_coiled.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":196,"uid":"aa803f43-b15e-4e1f-bb8a-e8830b803a90","name":"coiled - one tight coil around tegulum","type":0,"feature":80,"images":[{"caption":"Example full coil around the tegulum<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_tight_coil.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_tight_coil.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":197,"uid":"fc62764b-e244-4ca3-a53c-fc776bea8a0b","name":"coiled - two plus tight coils around tegulum","type":0,"feature":80,"images":[{"caption":"Example of two coils around tegulum","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_two_coils.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20form/embolus_form_two_coils.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":198,"uid":"0fb31457-83a4-46eb-aca8-2b7c158c4c58","name":"anticlockwise","type":0,"feature":81,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of emboli with an anti-clockwise curve<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"The embolus, whilst travelling in an anticlockwise direction in at least the terminal third, may be short or long and vary widely in complexity.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20direction%20of%20curve/embolus_curve_anticlockwise.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20direction%20of%20curve/embolus_curve_anticlockwise.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":199,"uid":"c2a48beb-04ee-48ef-b639-f895463bed74","name":"straight","type":0,"feature":81,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of emboli that are straight<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"The embolus, whilst straight, may be short or long and vary widely in complexity.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20direction%20of%20curve/embolus_curve_straight.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20direction%20of%20curve/embolus_curve_straight.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":200,"uid":"499b4a4c-b168-45d9-85a8-22fc8752e2cf","name":"clockwise","type":0,"feature":81,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of embolli with clockwise curves<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"The embolus, whilst travelling in a clockwise direction, may be short or long and vary widely in complexity.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20direction%20of%20curve/embolus_curve_clockwise.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20direction%20of%20curve/embolus_curve_clockwise.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":201,"uid":"41fdf673-0a20-4a66-8c71-4d237e2df577","name":"short thick","type":0,"feature":82,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of short thick emboli<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (AMS, QMB)","comments":"The embolus usually tapers along its length bur may be block-like in shape. Embolus length less than two-thirds length of tegulum.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_short_thick.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_short_thick.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":202,"uid":"d75ba4cb-1b74-4075-b4de-ff65bbcd7e42","name":"short thin","type":0,"feature":82,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of short thin emboli<br/>Illustrators (and &copy;) B.J. Richardson (T) (CSIRO, MP). M Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Embolus length less than two-thirds the length of the tegulum. Shape varies a great deal.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_short_thin.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_short_thin.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":203,"uid":"2b1cba7f-dec6-41d6-9acb-7621df09fab3","name":"long, ribbonlike","type":0,"feature":82,"images":[{"caption":"Example of a ribbon-like embolus<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_ribbonlike.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_ribbonlike.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":204,"uid":"f91cbdb2-493a-445d-a597-8a29614def15","name":"long, thin","type":0,"feature":82,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of long thin emboli<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"May be straight or curved. Length more than two-thirds the length of the tegulum","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_long_thin.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_long_thin.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":205,"uid":"8c3b8165-320c-49af-9fc1-6fda096cf1e7","name":"long thick","type":0,"feature":82,"images":[{"caption":"Example of a long thick emboli<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"May be straight or curved. Length more than two-thirds the length of the tegulum","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_long_thick.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/embolus%20shape/embolus_long_thick.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":206,"uid":"f2400e9f-f50f-4f98-bddb-1432c782aa24","name":"simple point","type":0,"feature":83,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of simple points to the embolus<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Simple, single points are the most commonly seen and can occur on all embolus forms and shapes","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20tip/Embolus_tip_simple.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20tip/Embolus_tip_simple.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":207,"uid":"1947c841-4519-40cc-a7af-2bdc68249c10","name":"bifurcate point","type":0,"feature":83,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of different bifurcate points to the embolus<br/>Illustrators (and &copy;) B.J. Richardson (L) (CSIRO), M. Zabka (TR) (QMB), B. Patoleta (BR) (MP)","comments":"Bifurcate or paired points on the tip of the embolus are usually close together in a simple terminal fork but can also be offset.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20tip/embolus_tip_bifurcate.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20tip/embolus_tip_bifurcate.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":208,"uid":"d3148723-dc6d-4077-8b5e-a54c2f6fcc7d","name":"distinct embolus and sclerite","type":0,"feature":83,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of the conductor separate from the embolus<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"A conductor distinct from the embolus, and having various forms, occurs in some genera.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20tip/embolus_tip_separate_conductor.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Embolus%20tip/embolus_tip_separate_conductor.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":209,"uid":"b5b8a719-ef9c-4a0b-9bca-21d2845875ab","name":"none","type":0,"feature":84,"images":[{"caption":"Example of a tibia without an apophysis<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_absent.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_absent.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":210,"uid":"c4ff259b-4afe-4135-b921-58312a20f7de","name":"single","type":0,"feature":84,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of tibia with a single apophysis<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Single apophyses arising from the palpal tibia can be short, long, sharply pointed, blunt or even rounded.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_single.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_single.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":211,"uid":"b3171a83-a8b0-4e8a-b759-c5598e24fd87","name":"single divided into two","type":0,"feature":84,"images":[{"caption":"Eamples of tibia with a single apophysis divided into two<br/>Illustrators (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB), B.J. Richardson (TR) (CSIRO, MP)","comments":"Two points of many types, including paired sharp points, two closely or widely offset points, two points paired as if bifurcate, massive separate bulky points and others.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_divided.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_divided.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":212,"uid":"dbed7de6-4454-4f58-b5dc-88002f8d20a8","name":"two","type":0,"feature":84,"images":[{"caption":"Example of tibia with two apophyses<br/>Illustrators (and &copy;) M. Zabka, B.J. Richardson (TR) (CSIRO, MP)","comments":"Two apophyses are those where two separate bases arise from the palpal tibia. They can be close together or widely separated.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_two.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_two.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":213,"uid":"6133b4be-d453-481b-b820-c2996807c993","name":"three or more","type":0,"feature":84,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of tibia with three apophyses<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Three apophyses are are those where three or more separate bases arise from the palpal tibia. They can be close together or widely separated.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_three.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Tibial%20apophysis%20number/apophysis_three.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":214,"uid":"84c8bd1e-1098-4956-b350-9b4027446957","name":"meandering","type":0,"feature":85,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of meandering seminal vesicles<br/>Illustrators (and &copy;) B.J. Richardson (TL BR), (CSIRO) M. Zabka (BL TR) (QMB)","comments":"The seminal vesicle or duct moves across the centre of the tegulum, not just around the edges.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Seminal%20resevoir%20course/resevoir_meandering.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Seminal%20resevoir%20course/resevoir_meandering.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":215,"uid":"8ddaac1f-6efb-4a56-8b60-d783ea31374b","name":"not meandering","type":0,"feature":85,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of  peripheral seminal vesicles<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"This is a very variable state - just around the edge in some fashion, nor across it.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Seminal%20resevoir%20course/resevoir_not_meandering.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Seminal%20resevoir%20course/resevoir_not_meandering.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":216,"uid":"10a7d56c-5861-453f-b049-2348488c6dbb","name":"absent","type":0,"feature":86,"images":[{"caption":"Example of tegula without lobes<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"When there is no tegular lobe the proximal margin can be rounded, somewhat squared off, or other shapes as long as they are without narrowing and extension into a lobe.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Posterior%20lobe/no%20lobe.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Posterior%20lobe/no%20lobe.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":217,"uid":"0a22ff26-39f0-43e3-bc0f-ee556b3f70fd","name":"present","type":0,"feature":86,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of tegula with posterior lobes<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Lobes on the proximal margin take the form of a narrowing which then straightens to a more or less bulbous point, sometimes constricting then flaring to a bulb-like feature.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Posterior%20lobe/with%20lobes.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Posterior%20lobe/with%20lobes.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":218,"uid":"737de2c6-ac74-411e-ac0e-1bd7a8e9a588","name":"absent","type":0,"feature":87,"images":[{"caption":"Palps without tegular sclerites<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Tegular sclerites sometimes, also called tegular apophyses or conductors, are hardened (sclerotised) sections of the tegulum taking the form of a wide variety of shapes, or hardened surfaces, including ridges, flanges, extensions, spurs and flattened, convex or concave parts. They may be found in a variety of different positions. They are absent in most genera.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Tegular%20sclerite/Plate%20tegular_sclerite_absent%20.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Tegular%20sclerite/Plate%20tegular_sclerite_absent%20.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":219,"uid":"86a516bb-c845-42d6-b1af-6323d8671145","name":"present","type":0,"feature":87,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of tegular sclerites<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Tegular sclerites sometimes, also called tegular apophyses or conductors, are hardened (sclerotised) sections of the tegulum taking the form of a wide variety of shapes, or hardened surfaces, including ridges, flanges, extensions, spurs and flattened, convex or concave parts. They may be found in a variety of different positions.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Tegular%20sclerite/Plate_tegular_sclerite_revised_3.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Tegular%20sclerite/Plate_tegular_sclerite_revised_3.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":220,"uid":"48c96669-7c15-4e89-8692-ffb9250ddd2e","name":"absent","type":0,"feature":88},{"id":221,"uid":"79a92faf-e421-4dc9-9f8f-094db47787f9","name":"present","type":0,"feature":88,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of cymbial apophyses<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Cymbial apophyses are outgrowths or extensions of the cymbium which can be many shapes, and may be flange-like, lobe-like, sharply pointed or blunt","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Cymbial%20apophysis/Cymbial%20apophysis%20present.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Cymbial%20apophysis/Cymbial%20apophysis%20present.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":222,"uid":"e57aff19-d7b3-4248-9fbc-98caf379e481","name":"absent","type":0,"feature":89,"images":[{"caption":"Example of a femur without a femoral apophysis<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Spines may be present but no apophyses","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Femoral%20apophysis/femoral_apophysis_absent.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Femoral%20apophysis/femoral_apophysis_absent.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":223,"uid":"23a63a59-bd1b-4a55-86af-97cdd9f8d13c","name":"present","type":0,"feature":89,"images":[{"caption":"Examples where a femoral apophysis is present<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;)  B.J. Richardson (CSIRO), M. Zabka (diag.) (AMS)","comments":"A femoral apophysis is an outgrowth of the pedipalp's femur, usually squat, strong and variously shaped, Note different shapes in different species. illustrated.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/Femoral%20apophysis/femoral_apophysis_present1.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/Femoral%20apophysis/femoral_apophysis_present1.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":224,"uid":"76f80439-722f-4c5e-bd4f-b221bd8fca4d","name":"acute and stiff; firmly joined","type":0,"feature":90,"images":[{"caption":"Example of a strongly flexed and stiff patella-femur joint<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (AMS)","comments":"The femur and patella are fused at the joint, and fixed at a sharp angle.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/FemoraL-tibia%20joint%20fixed/patella-femur_joint_fixed.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/FemoraL-tibia%20joint%20fixed/patella-femur_joint_fixed.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":225,"uid":"66a59272-309c-470d-9a62-a505598ef267","name":"flexible","type":0,"feature":90,"images":[{"caption":"Example of a flexible patella-femur joint<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Femur and patella connection in line with a relatively flexible joint.","path":"Images/features/Male%20palp/FemoraL-tibia%20joint%20fixed/patella-femur_joint_flexible.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Male%20palp/FemoraL-tibia%20joint%20fixed/patella-femur_joint_flexible.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":226,"uid":"7d9fe89e-b76f-407e-97ad-2259aabb669a","name":"Leg 3 longer","type":0,"feature":91},{"id":227,"uid":"424760e7-7a03-4795-970c-d447daa6cc10","name":"Leg 4 longer","type":0,"feature":91},{"id":228,"uid":"419a8626-fe75-46fd-abbd-2233fe75946a","name":"almost the same","type":0,"feature":91},{"id":229,"uid":"0403c6ba-ef15-4bc1-821a-d8b94305ed43","name":"relatively longer than tibia","type":0,"feature":92},{"id":230,"uid":"12ade958-3cfd-4b40-8e5c-5c3d0ac3ef4a","name":"relatively shorter than tibia","type":0,"feature":92,"images":[{"caption":"Diagram showing the measurements to use.<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Legs/tibia%20to%20femur%20lengths/femur_length.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/tibia%20to%20femur%20lengths/femur_length.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":231,"uid":"95af8bdb-477b-4e11-af85-87985b7ca239","name":"almost equal","type":0,"feature":92},{"id":232,"uid":"1039a34d-d1e6-4314-a8e1-b781fe47a5fa","name":"swollen: max. width >1/3 length","type":0,"feature":93,"images":[{"caption":"Femur of leg 1 relatively swollen<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka","path":"Images/features/Legs/femur%20shape/femur_shape_swollen.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/femur%20shape/femur_shape_swollen.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":233,"uid":"61823f30-5dac-403a-a60e-39eec7441f3b","name":"not  swollen","type":0,"feature":93,"images":[{"caption":"Femur of leg 1 relatively narrow<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Legs/femur%20shape/femur_shape_slim.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/femur%20shape/femur_shape_slim.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":234,"uid":"85c204f2-e98d-4690-a149-c6f294a60efc","name":"absent or very light","type":0,"feature":94,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of legs without any fringing<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) B.J. Richardson (CSIRO)","comments":"A fringe consists of a narrow band along the ventral (usually) or dorsal length of one or several leg segments; unlike hairs that are be distributed across the surface of the segment.","path":"Images/features/Legs/Fringe%20on%20L1/L1_no_fringe.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/Fringe%20on%20L1/L1_no_fringe.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":235,"uid":"a777c74f-6adc-499c-b25d-7c4be2cfb453","name":"present - sparse","type":0,"feature":94,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of legs with sparse fringing<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) B.J. Richardson (CSIRO)","comments":"A fringe consists of a narrow band along the ventral (usually) or dorsal length of one or several leg segments; unlike hairs that are be distributed across the surface of the segment.","path":"Images/features/Legs/Fringe%20on%20L1/L1_light_fringe.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/Fringe%20on%20L1/L1_light_fringe.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":236,"uid":"e91d5c6c-bbcd-45b2-890d-d7b2f6462d21","name":"present - thick","type":0,"feature":94,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of legs with thick fringing<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) B.J. Richardson (CSIRO)","comments":"A fringe consists of a narrow band along the ventral (usually) or dorsal length of one or several leg segments; unlike hairs that are be distributed across the surface of the segment.","path":"Images/features/Legs/Fringe%20on%20L1/L1_heavy_fringe.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/Fringe%20on%20L1/L1_heavy_fringe.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":237,"uid":"66e39a18-9f7e-4216-9aa3-cd3ca25e0203","name":"absent","type":0,"feature":95},{"id":238,"uid":"9d246a8a-9f7a-4a24-8d2e-8c0aafbbceb2","name":"1 or 2","type":0,"feature":95,"images":[{"caption":"Tibia with two spines<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Legs/tibial%20spines/tibia_spines_two.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/tibial%20spines/tibia_spines_two.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":239,"uid":"28908d63-24f9-4a86-a869-aa3decf1057f","name":"3, rarely 4","type":0,"feature":95,"images":[{"caption":"Tibia with three spines<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Legs/tibial%20spines/tibia_spines_three.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/tibial%20spines/tibia_spines_three.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":240,"uid":"7d88a1fc-83fc-4716-9b34-1b55a546b6c1","name":"5 (or more)","type":0,"feature":95,"images":[{"caption":"Tibia with five or more spines<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Legs/tibial%20spines/tibia_spines_five.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/tibial%20spines/tibia_spines_five.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":241,"uid":"83670af0-09c6-4568-8e85-6e435fbc8f00","name":"absent","type":0,"feature":96},{"id":242,"uid":"c016add8-c9c7-4d66-a7f6-5a0bf8f06b51","name":"poorly developed","type":0,"feature":96,"images":[{"caption":"Examples of specimens with weakly developed spines<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Weak or fine spines on the side of the tibia facing forwards.","path":"Images/features/Legs/tibial%20spine%20development/spine_size_weak.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/tibial%20spine%20development/spine_size_weak.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":243,"uid":"fb7aa30a-dc2e-4df1-b9c3-bc50d69a9cae","name":"well developed","type":0,"feature":96,"images":[{"caption":"Strong spines on the prolateral side<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","comments":"Spines on the side of the tibia facing forwards.","path":"Images/features/Legs/tibial%20spine%20development/spine_size_robust.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/tibial%20spine%20development/spine_size_robust.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":244,"uid":"35f3d600-5cf1-44b1-8b26-cf6fdb33a6de","name":"not flattened nor swollen","type":0,"feature":97,"images":[{"caption":"Example of a typical almost parallel-sided tibia<br/>Illustrator (and &copy;) M. Zabka (QMB)","path":"Images/features/Legs/Swollen%20tibia/tibia_shape_normal.jpg","thumb_path":"Thumbs/features/Legs/Swollen%20tibia/tibia_shape_normal.jpg","type":1}]},{"id":245,"uid":"d03886a6-bf2a-4152-85b7-c426506e1e94","name":"swollen","type":0,"fea